WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.045
<v Examiner>Two marks.

2
00:00:02.045 --> 00:00:05.599
<v Examiner>Identify one of the rights of medication administration,

3
00:00:05.599 --> 00:00:08.962
<v Examiner>and give an example of how it's applied in aged care.

4
00:00:09.412 --> 00:00:14.381
The course's eight rights: right patient, right medication, right dose, right time,

5
00:00:14.381 --> 00:00:18.930
right route, the right to refuse, right indication, and right documentation.

6
00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.804
Plus two checks: the expiry date, and allergies.

7
00:00:21.804 --> 00:00:22.941
Why do they matter?

8
00:00:22.941 --> 00:00:26.473
Her formative answer, which she said should be very simple:

9
00:00:26.473 --> 00:00:29.167
they decrease the risk of a medication error.

10
00:00:29.617 --> 00:00:32.867
Now one applied to aged care, from her own answer.

11
00:00:32.867 --> 00:00:35.727
Right person: identity checked with a photo,

12
00:00:35.727 --> 00:00:39.757
because a resident with dementia may not answer to their name.

13
00:00:39.757 --> 00:00:45.411
Or right dose: some normal ranges don't apply to the elderly, so start low and go slow.

14
00:00:45.411 --> 00:00:51.131
Or right route: if they struggle to swallow, check with them and talk to the prescriber.

15
00:00:51.131 --> 00:00:55.161
Or refusal: involve whānau and the enduring power of attorney.

16
00:00:55.611 --> 00:00:58.567
Her best-response answers, for the multi-choice.

17
00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:00.476
Can't swallow the large tablet?

18
00:01:00.476 --> 00:01:04.602
Give a liquid formulation if one's available, same drug, same dose,

19
00:01:04.602 --> 00:01:06.449
and check with the prescriber.

20
00:01:06.449 --> 00:01:09.898
Patient says leave my pills on the table while I shower?

21
00:01:09.898 --> 00:01:14.393
The answer starts with no: take it while I'm here, or I'll bring it back.

22
00:01:14.393 --> 00:01:15.255
Made an error?

23
00:01:15.255 --> 00:01:19.319
Tell the prescriber, document it, and complete an incident report.

24
00:01:19.319 --> 00:01:21.167
Don't ring the Poisons Centre.

25
00:01:21.167 --> 00:01:25.046
And if you can't read an order, call the prescriber to clarify.

26
00:01:25.046 --> 00:01:25.785
Never guess.

27
00:01:26.235 --> 00:01:32.398
Eight rights: patient, medication, dose, time, route, refuse, indication, documentation;

28
00:01:32.398 --> 00:01:35.060
plus two checks, expiry and allergies.

29
00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.301
They decrease medication errors.

30
00:01:37.301 --> 00:01:42.833
Aged care: photo identity, start low and go slow, liquid if they can't swallow,

31
00:01:42.833 --> 00:01:44.864
whānau and E P A for refusal.

32
00:01:44.864 --> 00:01:47.105
Never leave meds at the bedside.

33
00:01:47.105 --> 00:01:51.097
An error: tell the prescriber, document, incident report.
